## What we'll build

In this article, we'll walk through a simplified implementation of the a remote MCP protocol with OAuth for authentication. We'll assume that the user's OAuth always suceeds for now and understand the OAuth flow for remote MCP servers.

We'll do so in three steps

1. First, we'll create and initialise our Hono project using Alchemy
2. Then, we'll start implementing a single MCP endpoint to get a remote MCP working nicely
3. Lastly, we'll then implement a gated endpoint and show how we can use inspector to implement OAuth authentication

This is the first in a series of articles on implementing Agentic Search for Notion on Cloudflare. In future articles, we'll show how to use Alchemy to setup the other dependencies and use Better Auth to secure our server.

### Initialising our project

We'll start by creating a new Hono project using Alchemy and then we'll start implementing a single MCP endpoint to get a remote MCP working nicely. If you've never used Alchemy, the documentation is pretty good and you can check [the docs here](https://alchemy.run/getting-started/).

We can do so by using the following commands

```bash
bun init -y
bun add hono alchemy zod @modelcontextprotocol/sdk
rm -r -- Claude.md index.ts .cursor
touch alchemy.run.ts
touch worker.ts
```

Let's now modify our `alchemy.run.ts` file to initialise a worker. We'll show how to do a simple remote MCP for now which will have a simple greeting tool

```ts
import alchemy from "alchemy";
const app = await alchemy("my-first-app");

const worker = await Worker("api", {
  name: "api-worker",
  entrypoint: "./worker.ts",
});

console.log(`Worker deployed at ${worker.url}`);

await app.finalize();
```

We'll also update our `worker.ts` file with a simple hello world call

```ts
import { Hono, type Env } from "hono";
import type { HonoWorker } from "./alchemy.run";

const app = new Hono<{ Bindings: (typeof HonoWorker)["Env"] }>();

app.get("/", (c) => {
  return c.text("Hello Hono!");
});

export default app;
```

Now let's run our project in local development mode. We need to log in first to Alchemy and authorize it to create resources with our Cloudflare account. You can do so by running the following command:

```bash
alchemy login
```

Once you've logged in, you can run the following command to start the local development server. You can verify it's working by visiting `http://localhost:1337` in your browser.

```bash
ivanleo@Ivans-MacBook-Pro ~/D/c/mcp-cloudflare> bun alchemy dev
Alchemy (v0.65.0)
App: mcp
Phase: up
Stage: ivanleo
WARN Development mode is in beta. Please report any issues to https://github.com/sam-goodwin/alchemy/issues.
[creating]  sessionIds Creating Resource...
