[dev]       api Ready at http://localhost:1337
[creating]  api > url Creating Resource...
[created]   api > url Created Resource
[created]   api Created Resource
Worker deployed at http://localhost:1337
```

Now if you navigate to http://localhost:1337 in your browser, you should see "Hello Hono!" displayed. We've now got a working Hono application running on Alchemy, our next goal is to implement our first remote MCP.

### Our First Remote MCP

If you've never used a MCP before, I've written about them being [microservices in disguise](./simple-mcps.md) and why they're really invaluable for building [small automations](./mcps-for-tiny-automations.md).

Declaring one is relatively simple, here we'll start by defining a MCP server with a single tool which will just echo what we pass into it. We'll define this in a `mcp-server.ts` file.

```ts
import { McpServer } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js";
import { z } from "zod";

const server = new McpServer({
  name: "my-app",
  version: "1.0.0",
});
server.registerTool(
  "echo",
  {
    title: "Echo Tool",
    description: "Echo back the user input",
    // @ts-ignore
    inputSchema: { message: z.string() },
  },
  async ({ message }) => {
    return {
      content: [{ type: "text", text: message }],
    };
  }
);

export default server;
```

Let's now update our server to expose a new MCP endpoint. Since we want our remote MCP to be a stateless one, we don't need to handle sessions. Let's add our new `POST` endpoint to support this.

```ts
app.post("/mcp", async (c) => {
  const { req, res } = toReqRes(c.req.raw);
  const sessionId = c.req.header("mcp-session-id");
  let transport = getTransport(sessionId);

  if (!transport) {
    const newSessionId = uuidv4();
    transport = await createSession(newSessionId);
    res.setHeader("mcp-session-id", newSessionId);
  }

  const body = await c.req.json();
  await transport.handleRequest(req, res, body);

  return toFetchResponse(res);
});

app.get("/mcp", (c) => {
  return new Response("Method Not Allowed", {
    status: 405,
    headers: {
      Allow: "POST",
    },
  });
});
```
